7592186059756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059756 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059756 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186059565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059756 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059887 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059887 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059887 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059887 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059887 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059887 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059887 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix Using the Cofactor Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059887 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186060657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059887 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059887 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059887 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d6d1f64b-2ca1-5bc1-b04e-f255dfa6a0e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060154 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060154 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060154 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060154 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060154 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "882c4c5b-7c6a-5276-989d-a6c4749ae87b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060154 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =5|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5(0-2)+0+3(-2+3) \\\\  & =-10+0+3 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-15-0 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33}=\\frac{1}{-7}\\cdot (-15)=\\frac{15}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060657 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060657 #edb/ref 1061 16475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060657 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "40317ef1-e2cc-5e12-bdbe-2a42b66c8d16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060657 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060658 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060658 #edb/ref 1061 16476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060658 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fc1a94a9-263e-5e41-a86c-15959c3a9e6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060658 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060659 #edb/ref 1061 16477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060659 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "902e05e9-d18d-5cea-b49d-e1ac3fd78059" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060659 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060660 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060660 #edb/ref 1061 16478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060660 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8784f82-7077-5c6e-b26b-f9e36420e73c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060660 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060661 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060661 #edb/ref 1061 16479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060661 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cb81629b-755e-56a7-88e6-963569b9be54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060661 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060933 #edb/ref 1064 "We can calculate the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ using the following five-step algorithm.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant $\\det(A)$ of the matrix.\n\n**Step 2:** Form the **matrix $M$ of the minors** of $A,$ where each entry $a_{ij}$ of $A$ is replaced by its minor $M_{ij}\\mathbin{:}$\n   ![](images/14/14905267240a1e8eb7f54ae47b813c24aa5eb26415c419bbd0ec53425693309b.svg)\n\n**Step 3:** Create the **matrix $C$ of cofactors** by changing the sign of some entries of the matrix of the minors according to the **rule of alternating signs**:\n   \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}+ & - & + \\\\ - & + & - \\\\ + & - & +\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFor each entry of $M$, we change the sign if the corresponding sign in the pattern is \"$-$\". Otherwise, if the corresponding sign in the pattern is \"$+$\", then we don't change the entry.\n\nSo, the matrix of cofactors is given by\n\n![](images/a5/a57831b81711ce44dc46c2a3a91b18d8dbfe4078697805369097421351294d2e.svg)\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors, $C^T.$\n\n![](images/e9/e93df3291f387eaac0f6992b38e005f72b08b71b99c7cef8fe6a3b8f42662ebc.svg)\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix:\n   \n\n$$\nA^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{\\det ( A)}C^{T}\n$$\n\nIn other words, we divide each entry of the transpose of the matrix of cofactors by the determinant of the original matrix (which we computed in step 1).\n\nLet's see this in practice, in the next example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060933 #edb/ref 1065 3051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060933 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9c7ba5cd-b4b2-595b-bbad-dfbdac248280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060933 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060934 #edb/ref 1064 "We make the matrix $C$ of cofactors by changing the sign of alternate entries of the matrix $M$ of the minors. So, each entry $c_{ij}$ of $C$ equals\n\n$$\nc_{ij} = (-1)^{i+j} M_{ij},\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the minor of the entry $a_{ij}$ in the matrix $A.$\n\nWith this and the formula for the inverse, we can write the formula for each entry $a'_{ij}$ of the inverse matrix $A^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\na'_{ij} = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)}c^t_{ij}\n        = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)} c_{ji}\n$$\n\nHere, we used the fact that the entry $c^t_{ij}$ of $C^T$ is equal to the entry $c_{ji}$ of $C$ by the definition of the transpose." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060934 #edb/ref 1065 3052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060934 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "835c9ab0-dbce-5297-9a57-3978df178fa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060934 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating Individual Entries of the Matrix of Cofactors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285876 #edb/ref 1080 "15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285876 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285876 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7576155e-753f-578e-a4ec-c1e146863345" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285906 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{15}{7}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285906 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285906 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7804ba9f-5287-5069-b31b-fbfc92859355" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186286140 #edb/ref 1080 "-15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186286140 #edb